E('dinosaurs|dinosaur|t rex|tyrannosaurus', 'Dinosaurs were reptiles that dominated life on land for over 160 million years.', 'They lived from about 230 to 66 million years ago.', 'Their era is divided into the Triassic, Jurassic and Cretaceous periods.', 'Tyrannosaurus rex lived near the very end, about 68 to 66 million years ago.', 'Some, like Argentinosaurus, were among the largest land animals ever.', 'Birds are living descendants of dinosaurs.', 'Many dinosaurs had feathers.', 'An asteroid impact near Mexico about 66 million years ago wiped out the non-bird dinosaurs.')
E('asteroid impact|chicxulub|extinction of the dinosaurs', 'The Chicxulub impact was the asteroid strike that ended the age of dinosaurs.', 'It happened about 66 million years ago.', 'The asteroid was about 10 kilometres wide.', 'It hit what is now Mexico’s Yucatán Peninsula.', 'It caused fires, tsunamis and years of darkness.', 'About three-quarters of all species died out.', 'Mammals then spread and diversified.')
E('stone age|paleolithic|neolithic', 'The Stone Age was the long period when humans made tools mainly from stone.', 'It began over 3 million years ago and ended with the use of metals, around 3000 BC in many places.', 'It is divided into the Paleolithic (Old), Mesolithic (Middle) and Neolithic (New) Stone Ages.', 'Paleolithic people were hunter-gatherers.', 'They made cave paintings, like those at Lascaux in France.', 'The Neolithic began with farming, around 10,000 BC in the Middle East.', 'Farming led to permanent villages and eventually cities.')
E('ice age|last ice age|woolly mammoth|mammoths', 'An ice age is a long period when ice sheets cover large parts of the Earth.', 'The last glacial period ended about 11,700 years ago.', 'Ice sheets covered much of North America and northern Europe.', 'Sea levels were about 120 metres lower than today.', 'Woolly mammoths, cave bears and sabre-toothed cats lived then.', 'Some mammoths survived on Wrangel Island until about 4,000 years ago, after the pyramids were built.', 'Humans crossed from Asia to the Americas over a land bridge.')
E('early humans|human evolution|homo sapiens|neanderthals|neanderthal', 'Human evolution is the story of how modern humans developed from earlier species.', 'Homo sapiens appeared in Africa about 300,000 years ago.', 'Earlier relatives include Australopithecus, like the famous “Lucy”.', 'Homo erectus was the first to spread out of Africa, about 1.9 million years ago.', 'Neanderthals lived in Europe and Asia until about 40,000 years ago.', 'Many people today carry a little Neanderthal DNA.', 'Modern humans spread across the world from Africa.')
E('lucy|australopithecus', 'Lucy is a famous fossil of an early human relative, Australopithecus afarensis.', 'She lived about 3.2 million years ago.', 'She was found in Ethiopia in 1974.', 'She walked upright but had a small brain.', 'She was named after the Beatles song Lucy in the Sky with Diamonds.')
E('bronze age|iron age', 'The Bronze Age and Iron Age are periods named after the metals people used.', 'The Bronze Age began around 3300 BC in the Middle East; the Iron Age followed from about 1200 BC.', 'Bronze is a mix of copper and tin.', 'Bronze Age civilisations include Egypt, Mesopotamia and the Minoans.', 'Many Bronze Age societies collapsed around 1200 to 1150 BC.', 'Iron was more common than tin, so iron tools spread widely.')
E('lascaux|cave paintings', 'The Lascaux caves in France contain some of the most famous prehistoric paintings.', 'They are about 17,000 years old.', 'They show horses, bulls, deer and other animals.', 'They were discovered by teenagers in 1940.', 'The caves were closed to the public in 1963 to protect the art.')
E('zeus|greek gods|greek mythology|olympians|mount olympus', 'Zeus was the king of the gods in Greek mythology.', 'Greek myths were told from at least the 8th century BC.', 'He ruled from Mount Olympus and threw thunderbolts.', 'The twelve Olympians included Hera, Poseidon, Athena, Apollo, Artemis, Ares, Aphrodite, Hermes, Hephaestus, Demeter and Dionysus (or Hestia).', 'Zeus overthrew his father, Cronus, and the Titans.', 'The Romans called him Jupiter.', 'Greek myths still shape stories, art and language today.')
E('hercules|heracles|twelve labours', 'Heracles, called Hercules by the Romans, was the greatest hero of Greek myth.', 'His stories were told in ancient Greece from early times.', 'He was the son of Zeus and a mortal woman.', 'He performed twelve labours, like slaying the Nemean lion and the Hydra.', 'He was famed for his great strength.', 'He became a god after his death.')
E('odin|thor|norse mythology|norse gods|valhalla|loki', 'Norse mythology is the mythology of the Vikings and other Scandinavian peoples.', 'Most of what we know was written down in Iceland in the 13th century.', 'Odin was the chief god, wise and one-eyed.', 'Thor was the god of thunder, wielding the hammer Mjölnir.', 'Loki was a trickster god.', 'Warriors who died in battle went to Valhalla, Odin’s hall.', 'Ragnarök was the prophesied end of the world.')
E('egyptian gods|osiris|anubis|isis', 'The ancient Egyptians worshipped many gods.', 'Their religion lasted for around 3,000 years.', 'Ra was the sun god.', 'Osiris was god of the dead and the afterlife.', 'Anubis, with a jackal’s head, guided souls and protected tombs.', 'Isis was a powerful goddess of magic and motherhood.', 'The dead were judged by weighing their heart against a feather.')
E('medusa', 'Medusa was a monster in Greek myth, a Gorgon with snakes for hair.', 'Her story is told in ancient Greek myth.', 'Anyone who looked at her turned to stone.', 'The hero Perseus cut off her head, using a shield as a mirror.', 'The winged horse Pegasus sprang from her blood.')
E('atlantis', 'Atlantis is a legendary island described by Plato.', 'Plato wrote about it around 360 BC.', 'He said it sank into the sea in a single day and night.', 'It was probably invented as a moral story.', 'No evidence of a real Atlantis has been found.', 'The eruption of Thera near Crete may have inspired the idea.')
E('minoans|knossos|minotaur', 'The Minoans were a Bronze Age civilisation on the island of Crete.', 'They flourished from about 3000 to 1100 BC.', 'Their great palace was at Knossos.', 'They were named after the legendary King Minos.', 'In myth, the Minotaur, half man and half bull, lived in a labyrinth there.', 'Their script, Linear A, has not been deciphered.')
E('stonehenge builders|druids|celts', 'The Celts were peoples who lived across much of Europe in the Iron Age.', 'Celtic culture flourished from around 800 BC.', 'Druids were their priests and teachers.', 'They left few written records of their own.', 'Celtic languages survive as Irish, Scottish Gaelic, Welsh and Breton.', 'Stonehenge is far older than the Celts.')
E('phoenicians', 'The Phoenicians were seafaring traders from the coast of modern Lebanon.', 'They flourished from about 1500 to 300 BC.', 'They developed an alphabet that inspired Greek and Latin letters.', 'They founded Carthage.', 'They were famous for a costly purple dye.')
E('assyria|assyrians', 'Assyria was a powerful empire in ancient Mesopotamia.', 'It was at its height from the 9th to the 7th century BC.', 'Its capital was Nineveh.', 'It was famous for a ruthless army.', 'King Ashurbanipal built a great library.', 'It fell in 612 BC.')
E('babylon|nebuchadnezzar|hanging gardens', 'Babylon was a great city of ancient Mesopotamia.', 'It flourished under Hammurabi and later under Nebuchadnezzar II, who ruled from 605 to 562 BC.', 'Nebuchadnezzar rebuilt it with the blue Ishtar Gate.', 'He captured Jerusalem and exiled many Jews to Babylon.', 'The Hanging Gardens of Babylon may be a legend.', 'Cyrus the Great took the city in 539 BC.')
E('olmecs', 'The Olmecs were the earliest known major civilisation of Mexico.', 'They flourished from about 1200 to 400 BC.', 'They carved giant stone heads.', 'They may have played an early form of the Mesoamerican ballgame.', 'They influenced the Maya and later cultures.')
E('moai|easter island|rapa nui', 'The moai are giant stone statues on Easter Island, Rapa Nui, in the Pacific.', 'They were carved mostly between about 1250 and 1500.', 'There are nearly 1,000 of them.', 'They represent ancestors.', 'Islanders probably “walked” them upright with ropes.')
